able, now the checks for lookup join are split between here and directly in `Analyzer.resolveIndex`: https://github.com/elastic/elasticsearch/blob/e2f2ac35c2f91f101dc049a3f06a184c78c69873/x-pack/plugin/esql/src/main/java/org/elasticsearch/xpack/esql/analysis/Analyzer.java#L240-L256\r\n\r\nIf it\u0027s not too much work, could we consolidate the two?","I guess the check in `Analyzer` is generic (ie. doesn\u0027t refer to LOOKUP) only because it\u0027s easier and because we use IndexMode.LOOKUP only in LOOKUP JOIN.\r\nI\u0027ll move it to LookupJoin.","Yeah. I think it\u0027s actually clearer the way you moved it.\r\n\r\nThis relied on the fact that we assign `IndexMode.LOOKUP` at _parsing time_ to the `UnresolvedRelation` obtained from parsing `LOOKUP JOIN`. But that\u0027s more convoluted than your PR\u0027s current solution."],"path":"x-pack/plugin/esql/src/main/java/org/elasticsearch/xpack/esql/plan/logical/join/LookupJoin.java","id":37246,"created_at":"2025-01-22T18:15:12Z","original_position":34,"side":"RIGHT","position":35,"submitted_on_commit":"5d377a8b9227387f12effbeb89d93d1e243f6064","diff_hunk":"@@ -71,4 +75,14 @@ protected NodeInfo\u003cJoin\u003e info() {\n             config().rightFields()\n         );\n     }\n+\n+    @Override\n+    public void postAnalysisVerification(Failures failures) {","submitted_line":80},"generatedCommentsNodes":[]},{"groundTruth":{"original_line":83,"line":101,"comment_lines":["[][101]"],"review_comment":["nit: can we add a comment here that this check may be crucial for security, as we cannot deal with aliases adequately, yet?"],"path":"x-pack/plugin/esql/src/main/java/org/elasticsearch/xpack/esql/plan/logical/join/LookupJoin.java","id":37247,"created_at":"2025-01-22T18:19:25Z","original_position":37,"side":"RIGHT","position":55,"submitted_on_commit":"5d377a8b9227387f12effbeb89d93d1e243f6064","diff_hunk":"@@ -71,4 +75,14 @@ protected NodeInfo\u003cJoin\u003e info() {\n             config().rightFields()\n         );\n     }\n+\n+    @Override\n+    public void postAnalysisVerification(Failures failures) {\n+        super.postAnalysisVerification(failures);\n+        if (right() instanceof EsRelation esr) {\n+            if (esr.concreteIndices().contains(esr.indexPattern()) \u003d\u003d false) {","submitted_line":83},"generatedCommentsNodes":[]},{"groundTruth":{"original_line":83,"line":101,"comment_lines":["[][101]"],"review_comment":["Also: Could there be weird edge cases where the name of the index contains e.g. an escaped wildcard character, so that an index pattern _is_ being used, but coincidentally, it also matches a concrete index? There\u0027s no escaping in index names, right? Per the docs it doesn\u0027t seem so: https://www.elastic.co/guide/en/elasticsearch/reference/current/indices-create-index.html\r\n\r\nI understand this is as edge case-y as it gets; but since avoiding aliases is required to ensure permissions are respected correctly, we better try to think about every possible scenario."],"path":"x-pack/plugin/esql/src/main/java/org/elasticsearch/xpack/esql/plan/logical/join/LookupJoin.java","id":37248,"created_at":"2025-01-22T18:21:21Z","original_position":37,"side":"RIGHT","position":55,"submitted_on_commit":"5d377a8b9227387f12effbeb89d93d1e243f6064","diff_hunk":"@@ -71,4 +75,14 @@ protected NodeInfo\u003cJoin\u003e info() {\n             config().rightFields()\n         );\n     }\n+\n+    @Override\n+    public void postAnalysisVerification(Failures failures) {\n+        super.postAnalysisVerification(failures);\n+        if (right() instanceof EsRelation esr) {\n+            if (esr.concreteIndices().contains(esr.indexPattern()) \u003d\u003d false) {","submitted_line":83},"generatedCommentsNodes":[]}],"coveredGroundTruth":0,"uncoveredGroundTruth":3,"coveredMatchable":6,"uncoveredMatchable":4,"timing":14679,"tokensIn":6648,"tokensOut":647}